= Appendix A — Rapid Recall

Cover the answer after the dash and retrieve it before looking. These prompts supplement, rather than replace, the worked questions.

+ Planned activity intended to improve fitness — exercise.
+ Movement produced by skeletal muscle that uses energy — physical activity.
+ Learning through reasoning and understanding — cognitive learning.
+ Moving a limb towards the midline — adduction.
+ Moving a limb away from the midline — abduction.
+ The thigh bone — femur.
+ The kneecap — patella.
+ The upper-arm bone — humerus.
+ The forearm bones — radius and ulna.
+ The lower-leg bones — tibia and fibula.
+ Joint that permits movement in many directions at the shoulder — ball-and-socket.
+ Tissue connecting bone to bone — ligament.
+ Tissue connecting muscle to bone — tendon.
+ Muscle primarily producing a movement — agonist.
+ Muscle opposing a movement — antagonist.
+ Brain and spinal cord together — central nervous system.
+ Tiny lung structures where gas exchange occurs — alveoli.
+ Blood vessels carrying blood away from the heart — arteries.
+ Blood vessels carrying blood towards the heart — veins.
+ Vessel carrying blood from the left ventricle — aorta.
+ Blood component involved in clotting — platelets.
+ Oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells — haemoglobin.
+ FITT element describing how often — frequency.
+ FITT element describing how hard — intensity.
+ FITT element describing duration — time.
+ FITT element describing the activity — type.
+ Ability to sustain repeated muscle effort — muscular endurance.
+ Ability to exert force — muscular strength.
+ Ability to change direction effectively — agility.
+ Controlling body position — balance.
+ Training that reflects intended activity demands — specificity.
+ Gradually increasing suitable demands — progression.
+ Decline in some adaptations when training stops — reversibility.
+ Athletics event using a baton — relay.
+ Athletics event clearing a bar — high jump.
+ Athletics event measuring a leap into a landing area — long jump.
+ A putting event using a heavy implement — shot put.
+ Tournament in which entrants meet others according to a schedule — round robin.
+ Standard tournament eliminating a competitor after a loss — single knockout.
+ Respect for rules and opponents — fair play.
+ Time free from obligations — leisure.
+ Chosen activity for enjoyment or renewal — recreation.
+ Equipment and rule changes supporting access — adaptations.
+ Ask about needs rather than assuming them — person-centred inclusion.
